--- body ---
## Context

Decided by the author on 2026-11-07, in sen-causal-reading's design note
`conception/2026-11-07-citation-index.md` § 5, decision 1 (PR
MinhHaDuong/sen-causal-reading#137). The author chose to amend the rule now,
for every project, over a project exception.

`rules/zotero.md` says git holds neither the sources nor the `.bib`. Practice
differs: five of five surveyed repositories with a bibliography track one
(climate-finance-het `deliverables/_shared/bibliography/main.bib`, AEDIST
`report/refs.bib`, fuzzy-corpus, cadens, livre-milliards-climat). A tracked
`.bib` is what lets a fresh clone or a reader follow a citation: its DOI or
open-access URL is the reader route.

What the survey also showed, to carry into the rule:

- Tracking a `.bib` does not keep `file=` links alive: in climate-finance-het
  0 of 188 `file=` paths resolve on padme, and its test checks the field, not
  the file. A `file=` field is a local hint; the DOI or URL is what travels.
- A hand-kept `.bib` drifts (AEDIST header announces 65 entries, holds 108).
  The design note generates the bibliographic fields from Zotero through the
  local API and keeps project-owned fields (citekey, `ids`, `keywords`,
  `annotation`) without writing to the shared library.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `rules/zotero.md` allows a tracked `.bib`; sources (PDF, HTML, OCR
      output) stay out of git as before.
- [ ] The rule states that each cited entry carries a reader route (DOI or
      open-access / archived URL) and that `file=` is a local hint, never
      the reference.
- [ ] The zotero skill text that repeats "`.bib` is git-ignored"
      (`skills/zotero/SKILL.md`, `references/`) agrees with the rule.
- [ ] Any test or guard that enforces an ignored `.bib` is updated or
      removed.
